# Using your own Shader implementation

By default, the plugin provide a DefaultShader implementation but with version 1.6 you can use your own implementation.

Create a standard Maven project with your implementation. Make the shader a JSR-330 component, named by the hint that selects it:

```java
import javax.inject.Named;
import javax.inject.Singleton;

import org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.Shader;

@Singleton
@Named("mock")
public class MockShader implements Shader {
    // implement the interface here
}
```

The project needs a `javax.inject` dependency and the component index that the `sisu-maven-plugin` writes; see [How to use JSR-330](https://maven.apache.org/maven-jsr330.html#How_to_use_JSR-330).

Assuming your project has coordinate org.foo.bar:wine:1.0, you must add it as a dependency of the shade plugin.

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <dependencies>
          <dependency>
            <groupId>org.foo.bar</groupId>
            <artifactId>wine</artifactId>
            <version>1.0</version>
          </dependency>
        </dependencies>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <phase>package</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>shade</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <shaderHint>mock</shaderHint>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
  ...
</project>
```

Now the mojo will use your own implementation.
